WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.020
An organoid has no blood.

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.840
Every bit of oxygen must diffuse in from its surface.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.800
What happens at the center?

4
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.770
The cells use oxygen as it moves inward.

5
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:21.890
So it runs out a short way in.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.140
Take a simple model, with assumed rates of oxygen use.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.160
In a large organoid, oxygen reaches only about 0.1 to 0.6 millimetres in.

8
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:37.163
A large organoid is up to about 4 millimetres across.

9
00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:40.883
In this model, a third or more of it has no oxygen.

10
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:45.543
That is why cells in the center of a large organoid often die.

11
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:50.773
In adult cortex, capillaries sit only tens of micrometres apart.

12
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.767
Faster spinning helps only partly.

13
00:00:54.707 --> 00:00:58.827
It keeps the surface supplied, but under these assumptions it cannot feed a deep

14
00:00:58.827 --> 00:00:59.827
core.

15
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.587
Shortening the path helps.

16
00:01:02.547 --> 00:01:05.067
Slice the organoid, or grow it where air meets liquid.

17
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.147
Blood is surroundings.

18
00:01:11.207 --> 00:01:14.887
The rule flags this failure, and the estimate shows how large it is.

19
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.120
Now a finer test, cell by cell.

20
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.760
Do the organoid's cells match fetal cortex?

21
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.020
Single-cell RNA sequencing counts the messenger RNAs in thousands of separate cells.

22
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:32.133
Cells with similar profiles are grouped into clusters.

23
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:36.373
Then each organoid cluster is compared with each fetal one.

24
00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:42.717
In 2020, Aparna Bhaduri and colleagues compared hundreds of thousands of cells this

25
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:43.717
way.

26
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:48.607
The broad classes matched: radial glia, intermediate progenitors and neurons.

27
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:51.987
Those are program features, as the rule predicts.

28
00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:54.547
Finer subtypes blurred.

29
00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:59.907
An organoid cluster often resembled several fetal subtypes about equally.

30
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:06.287
Markers of particular subtypes, such as SATB2 in upper-layer neurons, were much

31
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:07.287
rarer.

32
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.320
Here is the twist.

33
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.880
Organoid cells expressed stress genes at higher levels.

34
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:19.133
Transplanted into mouse cortex, those stress genes fell.

35
00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:22.993
Some cells came to resemble fetal subtypes more closely.

36
00:02:24.017 --> 00:02:26.317
So the dish itself seems to add problems.

37
00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:29.047
The rule says where to look first.

38
00:02:29.887 --> 00:02:30.887
It is not a verdict.

39
00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:33.467
Every feature still has to be measured.
